# Line-ending policy.
#
# CommunityFinder is developed on Windows but its shell scripts run on Linux (the
# Docker images and server/docker's Linux build/test client). Line endings are not
# cosmetic there: with core.autocrlf=true (the Windows default) a checkout rewrites
# files to CRLF, and a shell script with CRLF fails on Linux with a baffling error
# — the shebang becomes "#!/usr/bin/env bash\r", so the kernel looks for an
# interpreter named "bash\r" and reports "bad interpreter: No such file or
# directory", or lines fail with "$'\r': command not found". (This bit the honuware
# clone; pinning it up front avoids a repeat.)
#
# Rules are limited to the file types where the ending actually changes behaviour,
# rather than a blanket `* text=auto`, so the policy stays explicit and predictable.

# Must be LF — these are read/executed on Linux.
*.sh        text eol=lf
Dockerfile  text eol=lf
*.yml       text eol=lf
*.yaml      text eol=lf
*.cmake     text eol=lf

# Must be CRLF — cmd.exe is the only consumer.
*.cmd       text eol=crlf
*.bat       text eol=crlf

# Binary: never touch.
*.pem       -text
